R2.  Lemma 6.1 ("eps decreases along every orbit") is FALSE on the G < 0 branch.
     Counterexample: alpha = (3+sqrt5)/2, P(55,21) = (3,1), both positive.
==============================================================================
  PASS  P = ((-3,8),(-8,21)) as stated   ((-3, 8), (-8, 21))
  PASS  P(55,21) = (3,1)   (3, 1)
  PASS  both states are positive
  PASS  eps INCREASES along the step -- Lemma 6.1 fails   eps(55,21) = 0.745442 < eps(3,1) = 0.774116
     (55, 21): Q = 1, eta = -0.008131, G = -2.999654
     (3, 1): Q = 1, eta = -0.145898, G = -2.888544
  PASS  the cause: G < 0 there, so |G| DEcreases as |eta| grows and eps = |Q|sqrt(D)/|G| grows
  PASS  on the G<0 branch eps >= sqrt(Delta)/|B| (here 0.745356), an explicit positive floor   floor = 0.745356
  -- effect on the code: verdict() selects the competitor of LARGEST PERIOD
     below H_B, justified by Lemma 6.1.  On a G<0 branch that is the state of
     LARGEST eps, so a blocker could in principle be missed.  Mitigation: every
     G<0 state has eps >= sqrt(Delta)/|B|, so it cannot block a candidate with
     eps below that floor.  Whether every evaluated candidate satisfied this is
     tested in R4 below.

